+<a id="the-session-filter-seat"></a>
+## The session filter seat
+
+`ctx.sessionController.registerSessionFilter(hooks)` claims the single seat of optional session access hooks: a second registration throws because two access policies cannot compose, and the returned disposer releases the seat. Each callback is independent, and an absent callback leaves its seam byte-identical to an unconfigured deployment. `listFilter(principal, items)` rewrites the rows of `list` and the items of `search`; the generic preserves each item type, so summaries and search hits keep their own fields. `accessCheck(principal, sessionId)` admits every Session-addressed method: prompt, cancel, rename, selectModel, attachment, updateQueue, page, fork sources, and follow openings (a subagent address is checked against the child Session it names). `onSessionCreated(principal, sessionId)` is awaited after each successful create or fork, so its claims settle before the response; its rejection is logged and never fails the settled response.
+
+The principal is the request-scoped identity the webserver guard attached to the current request, opaque to the Host. With the matching callback registered, a request without a principal is denied, and a hook that denies or throws is a denial (`logger.warn` on a throw); an anonymous create is rejected before any creation side effect. Every denial is a `TypertRemoteFailure` with the stable code `forbidden`, passed through unchanged by every carrier and consumed by clients with HTTP 403 semantics, plus `{ sessionId }` details where a Session is addressed. `search.hasMore` still describes the provider page before filtering, never the filtered view. `ctx.sessionController.assertSessionAccess(sessionId)` exposes the same admission verdict for any future direct route that addresses a Session.
+
+-----
+
+<a id="the-control-frame-filter-seat"></a>
+## The control frame filter seat
+
+`ctx.sessionController.registerControlFrameFilter(factory)` claims the single per-stream control frame-filter seat: a second registration throws because two filters cannot compose, and the returned disposer releases the seat for later generations. When a `session/control` stream generation opens, the controller calls the factory once inside that open's request-principal continuation and stores the returned filter on the stream; broadcast paths run outside any connection's request context, so every later judgment reuses this open-time value, and a carrier without a guard principal builds its filter from `undefined`. The filter is synchronous — `(sessionId, frameType) => boolean`, with `frameType` one of `'baseline'`, `'queue'`, `'jobs'`, or `'projection'` — because baseline building and broadcast queue pushes are synchronous paths.
+
+Every control frame is Session-referenced and judged at three points. The opening baseline judges the Session list once per Session as `'baseline'`, so the queues, jobs, and projections records share one key set. Live `queue`, `jobs`, and `projection` broadcasts are judged per stream with the frame's own type. A `session/created` notification broadcasts the new Session's jobs frame through the same per-stream judgment. A filter that throws drops that frame for that stream with `logger.warn` (a baseline throw excludes that one Session) and never interrupts the broadcast loop or the stream. With no seat claimed, every stream receives control frames byte-identical to an unconfigured deployment.
+
+`SESSION_CONTROLLER_SESSION_REFERENCES` (from the `remote-events` subpath) declares the Session-reference extractors for the `api-session/*` notifications this package emits: `api-session/added` reads the summary's `sessionId` field, and the other four read their first argument. The Remote assembly hands the table to the Gateway's forwarded-event registration; the argument shapes belong to this emitter, so the Gateway stays free of these event names. Extractors return `undefined` for non-matching arguments and never throw — a throwing extractor would fail the whole event source.
+
+-----
